## Build requirements

Deliver a complete, polished, playable game, not a tech demo. The **hard
requirements** — real graphics drawn in code (no art files or runtime fetches),
no backend or credentials, the palette and type, a `README.md`, and the game
states — are stated in full in `specs/overview.md` (and the files it points to).
That specification is authoritative; build to it rather than to a summary here.
Two things bear restating, because the harness depends on them:

- **The fixed build/serve interface.** The project stays a **Node project** built
  with only Node.js and npm-installed dependencies. Extend the provided
  `package.json` at `{{workspace}}` and **commit a `package-lock.json`** (run
  `npm install` after you change dependencies so the lockfile matches). `npm ci`
  then `npm run build` must produce the static site — with no other manual step —
  into one of `dist/`, `build/`, or `out/` at the project root, with an
  `index.html` at the root of that directory. Serving that directory as-is at the
  root of any static file server must run the game; it is deployed to static
  hosting exactly that way. `specs/overview.md` states this in full; follow it
  exactly.
- **The reference screenshots.** The `reference/` folder under `{{workspace}}` holds
  screenshots that are visual targets for the title, gameplay, and win
  screens — match their layout, palette, and type, but build the screens from the
  specification rather than copying the images.

## Verify before you finish

Your project has **Playwright** and a headless **Chromium** installed as project
dependencies, plus network access — use them to verify your build, do not just
assume it works. Before you consider the task complete:

- Produce the **static production build** and serve it locally, then open it in
  Chromium with Playwright and confirm it **loads with no console errors** and no
  uncaught exceptions.
- **Drive the game** — deal a game, turn the stock, move cards between piles, send
  cards to the foundations, and reach a won game so the victory cascade plays —
  and **screenshot** the title, gameplay, and win screens. Compare each against
  the matching image in `reference/` and against the specs, and fix what does not
  match before finishing.
- **Capture the proof of implementation** exactly as `specs/proof.md` specifies:
  the screenshots and the short cascade clip, each written to the precise `proof/`
  path that spec names. These are required deliverables of the finished build, not
  a private check. Do not invent a different folder or filename, and do not skip
  the `cascade.webm`.

Use the project's own Playwright: import it from your project (for example
`import { chromium } from "playwright"`) or run the project-local CLI with `npx
playwright`. There is **no** global Playwright on `PATH` — it lives in your
project's `node_modules`, like any other dependency. Launch Chromium with the
`--no-sandbox` argument — this container has no Chromium sandbox, so a default
launch will fail.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a **git repository** — it was initialized before you started,
with the starter files in its first commit. Your finished implementation is
published directly from this repository's commits, so work you leave uncommitted
is **not** published. When the build is complete and verified, commit everything:
run `git add -A` then `git commit`, and confirm `git status` reports a clean
working tree with nothing uncommitted or untracked. A `.gitignore` already
excludes `node_modules/` and the build output (`dist/`/`build/`/`out/`), so a
clean tree does not mean committing those — but it does mean committing your
source, your `package-lock.json`, your `README.md`, and the `proof/` artifacts.
